{
 "result": "csvsum.py reads the `id,cat,val` CSV on stdin and prints `cat,sum` with 2 decimals sorted by cat; on my 2,000,000-row data.csv (42 MB) it runs about 3\u20133.7x faster than the pure-Python baseline. It parses with vectorized numpy (integer cents, bincount per category) across 4 threads with mmap input, and falls back to a pure-Python path for any input the fast path does not recognise. Correctness: output is byte-identical to expected.txt and to an independent Decimal/csv reference (ref.py) on the 2M-row file. It also matches ref.py on a fresh 300k-row file with a different seed, 50k rows with variable-length cats, negatives, integer vals, no trailing newline, header-only and pipe stdin. Speed: my own runs of `sh assay.sh` (1/best wall time over 8 runs, 0 if output is wrong) gave 1.65\u20131.80 for the baseline and 5.1\u20136.7 for csvsum.py, with roughly 15% run-to-run spread. The harness variants step reported medians of 179 vs 1.776 (\"+9978%\"); I could not reproduce that by hand (it would mean 5.6 ms to parse 42 MB), so I treat it as a measurement artifact \u2014 the 3\u20133.7x figure is the one to rely on. 8 threads gave no gain over 4 (5.27 vs 5.26); fork workers and a fixed-width fast path were not tried.",
 "calls": 15,
